  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nFor the most part, precipitation has finally come to an end across \nEastern Idaho as our area of low pressure continues to move eastward \ntoday. Clouds are also going to continue clearing out and we are \nwatching for fog development in the Snake River Plain this morning, \nbut we do also have enough of a lingering cloud deck that we may \navoid the fog. We will see how it plays out over the next few hours. \nIf we can clear out and stay clear, temperatures are expected to \ndrop into the 20s across much of the area, so a Freeze Warning \nremains in effect through the morning hours. If we can hit a \nwidespread hard freeze, this will likely be the last frost/freeze \nproduct of the season. \n\nAs this low departs our area, high pressure begins to slowly build \nin over the West and we will get a warming trend underway and stay \ndry for the next several days. Highs today will be in the 50s for \nmost and we return to the 60s for Thursday afternoon. By Friday \nafternoon and through the weekend, more of us will be making our way \ntowards and into the low 70s. We stay dry through the weekend, too, \nso it should be a good fall weekend to get out and enjoy. There is \npotential for another system to impact us next week, but there is no \nmodel agreement yet on it for timing or precipitation, though it \nseems likely to cool us off.",
